{
  "dom_id": "step-q10435",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}=9.$",
  "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, we divide both sides of the equation by $9$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{9}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $a=\\sqrt{9}=3$ and $b=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{1}x \\\\  & =\\pm 3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
